1
00:00:00,220 --> 00:00:01,940
Cent #10000.

2
00:00:03,279 --> 00:00:06,080
Its number is also how many Cents there are in all.

3
00:00:07,139 --> 00:00:09,160
A penny struck in 1976

4
00:00:09,199 --> 00:00:10,119
with no mint mark,

5
00:00:10,400 --> 00:00:12,359
generally indicating the Philadelphia mint.

6
00:00:13,259 --> 00:00:15,519
Cent #10000 carries two traits,

7
00:00:15,820 --> 00:00:17,379
and the rarer is Assassin.

8
00:00:18,379 --> 00:00:19,760
The Cents came with no traits,

9
00:00:20,039 --> 00:00:21,519
only a year and a mint mark.

10
00:00:21,940 --> 00:00:23,260
In the words of Steven Reiss,

11
00:00:23,359 --> 00:00:26,000
in his article "Materiality and Signification":

12
00:00:26,359 --> 00:00:28,219
"The community therefore began naming

13
00:00:28,260 --> 00:00:29,800
the spontaneous variations.

14
00:00:30,199 --> 00:00:31,120
Traits like 'Ghost',

15
00:00:31,480 --> 00:00:33,520
'Faceless', or 'Assassin' were born."

16
00:00:34,840 --> 00:00:35,719
In the Traits Atlas,

17
00:00:35,960 --> 00:00:38,020
Assassin is "prominent spherical

18
00:00:38,079 --> 00:00:41,280
obsidian, verdigris or contamination striking Abe's

19
00:00:41,340 --> 00:00:42,859
head, neck, or torso –

20
00:00:43,280 --> 00:00:45,119
the mark of an imagined projectile."

21
00:00:46,240 --> 00:00:48,039
Here it is a single green spot

22
00:00:48,079 --> 00:00:49,340
at the back of Lincoln's neck.

23
00:00:50,320 --> 00:00:52,140
42 Cents carry Assassin.

24
00:00:53,939 --> 00:00:56,039
Two of them were struck in 1976.

25
00:00:57,259 --> 00:00:59,280
The second trait is Notable Verdigris:

26
00:00:59,700 --> 00:01:01,939
in the Atlas, "localised but clearly

27
00:01:01,979 --> 00:01:03,619
visible patches or films".

28
00:01:04,340 --> 00:01:05,680
On Cent #10000,

29
00:01:05,879 --> 00:01:07,620
the spot itself is verdigris.

30
00:01:08,980 --> 00:01:11,260
Cent #1256 was struck the same

31
00:01:11,340 --> 00:01:12,879
year, and is close to mint state.

32
00:01:13,599 --> 00:01:14,939
Its portrait is unmarked.

33
00:01:16,019 --> 00:01:18,500
As money, both meant exactly one cent.

34
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:20,799
An open signifier.

35
00:01:22,340 --> 00:01:24,519
Steven Reiss again: "Their former economic

36
00:01:24,599 --> 00:01:26,900
meaning is unhooked from the object and turned

37
00:01:26,939 --> 00:01:29,180
into a vessel for socially agreed value.

38
00:01:30,140 --> 00:01:32,000
The penny becomes an open signifier

39
00:01:32,079 --> 00:01:33,760
that can point to many signifieds

40
00:01:33,760 --> 00:01:37,219
- artistic, collector, historical, crypto."

41
00:01:38,560 --> 00:01:39,680
The portrait is Abraham

42
00:01:39,739 --> 00:01:43,959
Lincoln, assassinated in 1865. In the Atlas,

43
00:01:44,239 --> 00:01:46,060
the Assassin's "singular placement

44
00:01:46,180 --> 00:01:48,239
transforms decay into narrative".

45
00:01:49,579 --> 00:01:51,819
In 1968, long before CENTS,

46
00:01:52,140 --> 00:01:54,040
Lucy Lippard and John Chandler wrote,

47
00:01:54,099 --> 00:01:56,000
in their essay "The Dematerialization

48
00:01:56,040 --> 00:01:57,439
of Art": "What something

49
00:01:57,519 --> 00:01:59,200
looks like and what it is about

50
00:01:59,400 --> 00:02:00,939
may be complementary but not

51
00:02:00,959 --> 00:02:03,480
necessarily (rarely) identical."

52
00:02:04,799 --> 00:02:08,120
To look at, Cent #10000 is a 1976

53
00:02:08,180 --> 00:02:09,659
penny with one green spot.

54
00:02:10,460 --> 00:02:12,340
What it means, and what it is worth,

55
00:02:12,659 --> 00:02:14,819
the people who collect CENTS keep deciding.

56
00:02:16,379 --> 00:02:19,099
Cent #10000, a great Cent.
